//! The window host's render thread. It owns the GPU: the Dawn instance, the device, the window's
//! surface, the renderer and the atlas textures, all created on that thread, so no GPU object
//! crosses threads. The UI thread lays out and paints as before, then hands each finished frame
//! over with [`RenderThread.submit`]: a copy of the scene, the atlas texture list and the new
//! atlas pixels. The render thread uploads, waits for a drawable, draws and presents, so waiting
//! for the display never blocks input.
//!
//! The hand-over is a one-slot mailbox where the newest frame wins. A frame the render thread
//! has not taken yet is replaced, and its uploads are kept in front of the new frame's, so no
//! atlas pixels are lost. The replaced scene's storage is reused for the next copy.
//!
//! The render thread also scrolls on its own, as a browser compositor does: the window host
//! hands each frame's [`FastScroll`] (which scrollers the user may scroll, and where wheel
//! listeners are) with it, and the platform thread scrolls through a [`Compositor`] when a wheel
//! event can go no further than a scroller. The render thread draws the last frame again with the
//! new offsets and the UI thread learns them afterwards. Offsets a frame of the UI thread already
//! took ([`RenderThread.submit`]'s `fastSeq`) are dropped.
//!
//! Windows past the first draw on the same thread, device and atlas textures, each through a
//! surface of its own ([`RenderThread.openSurface`], [`RenderThread.submitTo`]); the compositor
//! scrolls the first window only. Atlas pixels do not travel with a frame: every window's
//! uploads join one queue, in the order the frames were painted, and the render thread writes
//! them before it draws anything, so a window has the tiles another window's frame uploaded.
//!
//! Everything a frame carries lives in the process heap, never in a caller's arena: the copies
//! are made with the system allocator, whatever allocator the caller provides.
import ui_scene.scene { Scene }
import ui_scene.scroll { Placements, ScrollGroup, ScrollPosition, UNBOUNDED }
import ui_tree.node { Move }
